    "conversation_id": "6f11a6b1-d2f5-492c-a31f-6eeeb5baa4de",
    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **No, you should not make an issue of the panel's gender composition, nor is it, on its own, a sign of bias or improper procedure.**\n\nHere is an objective breakdown of why, along with a realistic look at how academic hiring decisions work and what you should do next.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Composition Is Not Evidence of Bias\n* **Procedural reality:** Selection committees are typically assembled based on availability, specific departmental roles (e.g., head of department, director of teaching, external examiner), or committee rotation schedules. \n* **The burden of proof:** Just as an all-male panel is not automatic legal or procedural evidence of bias against female candidates, an all-female panel is not evidence of bias against male candidates. \n* **Professional risk:** Formally raising or implying gender bias solely because the panel and the successful candidate were female—without documentation of explicit discriminatory comments or procedural breaches—will almost certainly be dismissed. In academia, where subfields are small, doing so can carry significant reputational risk.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why CV Metrics Do Not Tell the Whole Story\nOnce you make the shortlist of three, the committee has already determined that all candidates meet the baseline qualifications. At that stage, pure publication counts and PhD status often matter less than other criteria:\n\n* **Strategic fit and curriculum needs:** The announcement specifically cited *\"strong experience from industry.\"* Modern economics departments—especially at public institutions—frequently face pressure to improve student employability, industry partnerships, or accreditation. A candidate with industry connections may fill an immediate curriculum or placement gap that a traditional academic profile does not.\n* **The job talk and interview performance:** Two rounds of interviews and a job talk carry enormous weight. Committees look closely at collegiality, communication style, alignment with the department’s teaching philosophy, and how well the candidate answered tough questions.\n* **Paper quality vs. volume:** In economics, two top-tier placements (e.g., top 5 or leading field journals) or a stellar job-market paper can sometimes carry more weight than six papers in lower-tier journals.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay for Feedback\n* **Academic administrative pace:** Two weeks is very short in university administration. Search committees often do not release formal feedback until the chosen candidate has officially signed the contract, completed background checks, and HR has formally closed the recruitment file.\n* **HR constraints:** Many public universities have strict policies limiting what feedback can be shared in writing to protect against litigation, meaning responses must often be cleared through HR or legal counsel before being sent.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Do not mention the gender makeup of the panel.** It will detract from any legitimate request for feedback and immediately put the department on the defensive.\n2. **Follow up politely for developmental feedback:** If you hear nothing after another week (around three weeks from your initial email), send a brief, courteous follow-up to the committee chair:\n   > *\"I understand the committee has a heavy workload and the recruitment process takes time to wrap up. If possible, I would still welcome any brief feedback on my interview and job talk that could help me strengthen future applications.\"*\n3. **Focus on areas you can control:** If you receive feedback, use it to understand what the department felt was missing (e.g., applied industry context, specific teaching methodologies, or research pipeline articulation).\n",
